## Pinned Intel TDX material: what it is, where every byte came from, and
## which of it is real.
##
## ## The short version, because this is the thing a reader has to know
##
## **Every positive fixture here is genuine hardware output, and that is
## established cryptographically rather than by filename.** Three
## attestation quotes, produced by three different Intel parts, each
## carrying its own Provisioning Certification Key chain. Each one was
## verified end to end — before a line of the code under test was
## written — with an implementation sharing no code with this one: a
## from-scratch pure-Python ECDSA P-256 verifier over a from-scratch DER
## reader, self-tested against RFC 6979's P-256/SHA-256 vector. For each
## quote, all six of these hold:
##
##   * the PCK leaf verifies under the PCK CA, which verifies under the
##     Intel SGX Root CA, which verifies under itself;
##   * that root is **byte-identical** to the one Intel's own
##     certificate service serves today;
##   * the quoting enclave's report verifies under the PCK leaf's key;
##   * the 32 bytes of that report's report data equal
##     `SHA-256(attestation key ‖ QE authentication data)`;
##   * the quote's own signature verifies under that attestation key,
##     over the header and the TD report exactly as they arrived; and
##   * a single bit flipped anywhere in the signed span breaks it.
##
## The private half of the key at the end of every one of those chains
## exists in exactly one place, and it is not this repository.
##
## **Two things here are NOT genuine, and both are negatives.** One was
## published by Intel, the other was minted here. Both exist to be
## refused, and the file says which is which at every point.
##
## Nothing positive in this file was signed by the code the gates
## exercise.
##
## ## The root, and why its corroboration is unusually strong
##
## `IntelSgxRootCaDerHex` is 659 bytes, sha256
## `44a0196b2b99f889b8e149e95b807a350e7424964399e885a7cbb8ccfab674d3`.
## The same 659 bytes arrive by **five independent routes**, all checked
## here rather than assumed:
##
##   | route | fetched |
##   |-------+---------|
##   | `certificates.trustedservices.intel.com/Intel_SGX_Provisioning_Certification_RootCA.cer` | live, 2026-09-21 |
##   | the `TCB-Info-Issuer-Chain` header of Intel's TCB service | live, same session |
##   | the `SGX-Enclave-Identity-Issuer-Chain` header of Intel's enclave-identity service | live, same session |
##   | the `SGX-PCK-CRL-Issuer-Chain` header of Intel's revocation service | live, same session |
##   | the certification data **inside all three genuine quotes below**, published by four unrelated projects | at their pinned commits |
##
## Twelve further open-source projects check the same DER into their own
## trees; that was surveyed rather than relied on, and the five routes
## above are the ones this file's claims rest on.
##
## One correction worth carrying, because the obvious URL is a trap:
## `certificates.trustedservices.intel.com/IntelSGXRootCA.der` is **not**
## the root certificate. Its body decodes to a `TBSCertList` with
## `version v2` — it is the root's revocation LIST, and it is pinned here
## as `IntelRootCrlDerHex`. There is no keyless URL that serves the root
## certificate on its own; the certificate arrives in the issuer-chain
## headers and inside the quotes.
##
## ## The three genuine quotes
##
## | constant | bytes | published by | what it is |
## |----------+-------+--------------+------------|
## | `GoTdxGuestSprQuoteHex` | 4,974 | `google/go-tdx-guest` `testing/testdata/tdx_prod_quote_SPR_E4.dat` @ `48f3644c` | a version-4 quote from a Sapphire Rapids part, FMSPC `50806f000000` |
## | `GoTdxGuestEmrQuoteHex` | 5,006 | `google/go-tdx-guest` `testing/testdata/quote_sample_v5.dat` @ `48f3644c` | a version-5 quote, FMSPC `90c06f000000` |
## | `TrusteeV5QuoteHex` | 5,006 | `confidential-containers/trustee` `deps/verifier/test_data/tdx_quote_5.dat` **and** `automata-network/automata-dcap-attestation` `evm/forge-test/assets/quotes/tdx_quote_5.dat` | a version-5 quote from a third part |
##
## Two qualifications, both measured rather than assumed.
##
## **The first file is not 4,974 bytes of quote.** Its quote declares a
## signature-data length that ends at byte 4,935, and the 39 bytes after
## it are the ASCII text `"\nextra bytes(only for testing purpose)\n"`,
## which the publisher appends deliberately. So the genuine document is
## the 4,935-byte prefix, sha256
## `3507b5f7e6124e17210ffb4d5caf25a5d289a64fb19068ae90cd4cb25828db9f`,
## and **a second publisher checks in exactly those 4,935 bytes**:
## `oasisprotocol/oasis-core` `go/common/sgx/pcs/testdata/quote_v4_tdx_ecdsa_p256_out_of_date.bin`
## @ `6d2f95be`. The whole 4,974-byte file is pinned, not the prefix,
## because the 39 bytes are themselves a fixture: a published document
## carrying bytes past the end of what it declares is the input the
## trailing-bytes rule needs, and it did not have to be manufactured.
##
## **`TrusteeV5QuoteHex` has two independent publishers and
## `GoTdxGuestEmrQuoteHex` has one.** The two trustee/automata copies are
## byte-identical to each other and neither is a copy of the
## go-tdx-guest file. That asymmetry is stated rather than averaged away.
##
## The three parts are genuinely three. Their PCK certificates carry
## three different platform instance identifiers, two different FMSPCs
## and different platform TCB component levels; their TD reports carry
## three different `MRTD`s and two different `MRSEAM`s. Nothing
## fabricates five independent disagreements by accident.
##
## ## The collateral, and the two vintages
##
## Seven Intel-signed collateral documents are pinned, and **all seven
## were shown to verify** under the P-256 key in `Intel SGX TCB Signing`
## — the certificate Intel's service serves in its issuer-chain headers
## today — by the same from-scratch implementation, over the exact bytes
## of the signed member located by brace matching and never
## re-serialised.
##
##   | constant | bytes | source | issued |
##   |----------+-------+--------+--------|
##   | `GtgTcbInfoSprJson` | 2,401 | `google/go-tdx-guest` `sample_tcbInfo_response` | 2023-06-18 |
##   | `GtgTcbInfoEmrJson` | 4,268 | `google/go-tdx-guest` `sample_tcbinfo_response_v5` | 2026-02-01 |
##   | `GtgQeIdentityJson` | 624 | `google/go-tdx-guest` `sample_qeIdentity_response` | 2023-06-08 |
##   | `PcsTcbInfoSprJson` | 7,953 | Intel's TCB service, FMSPC `50806f000000` | 2026-09-21 |
##   | `PcsTcbInfoEmrJson` | 5,679 | Intel's TCB service, FMSPC `90c06f000000` | 2026-09-21 |
##   | `PcsTdxQeIdentityJson` | 624 | Intel's TDX enclave-identity service | 2026-09-21 |
##   | `PcsSgxQeIdentityJson` | 1,380 | Intel's **SGX** enclave-identity service | 2026-09-21 |
##
## The 2023 documents verifying under a certificate fetched in 2026 is a
## fact about Intel rather than about this file: the TCB signing key has
## not been rotated in that interval. It was worth checking, because a
## mirrored fixture whose signer had rotated would be unverifiable here
## and would have had to be labelled as such.
##
## Two of these are in the corpus for what they are NOT. `PcsSgxQeIdentityJson`
## states `id` `QE` and `isvprodid` 1, where a TDX quoting enclave's
## identity states `TD_QE` and 2 — a genuine, vendor-signed document that
## is the wrong document, which is the input the identity-mismatch rule
## needs. It is also the only one of the seven whose TCB level list is
## not degenerate: six levels, five of them `OutOfDate`, against one
## level apiece in the two TDX identities.
##
## ## What the corpus is NOT degenerate in, stated because a corpus
## ## that is single-anything has defeated this tree before
##
##   * two quote versions (4 and 5) and two TD report body shapes (584
##     bytes, and the 648-byte one that carries a second TCB SVN array
##     and a service-domain measurement);
##   * two FMSPCs across three parts;
##   * four TCB information documents with **2, 3, 4 and 6** TCB levels
##     — so a walk that stopped after the first level, or that never
##     reached a second, is visible;
##   * both branches of the TDX module rule: the Sapphire Rapids quote's
##     module major version is 0 and both version-5 quotes' is 1, and
##     that number decides whether two components of the level
##     comparison are skipped and whether a separate module identity is
##     consulted at all;
##   * both outcomes of the platform walk: the Sapphire Rapids part is
##     below **every** level Intel publishes for its FMSPC, under both
##     vintages, and the version-5 parts are at the top level under both;
##   * a revocation list that actually revokes something — Intel's
##     platform list names a serial — and one that revokes nothing.
##
## ## The revocation lists
##
## `PcsPckCrlPlatformDerHex`, `PcsPckCrlProcessorDerHex` and
## `IntelRootCrlDerHex` are the verbatim bodies of Intel's three
## revocation endpoints, fetched 2026-09-21. They expire: the two PCK
## lists state a next update of 2026-10-21 and the root's of 2027-02-26.
## Every case that consults one therefore fixes its own clock rather than
## reading the host's, for the same reason the vendor material in this
## tree's other confidential-computing corpus does — a gate that read the
## real time would begin failing on a date nobody chose.
##
## The processor list is in the corpus because it is issued by a
## different certificate authority than the one the pinned chains run
## through, which is the input the "this list does not answer the
## question" rule needs, and it is a real vendor document rather than a
## manufactured one.
##
## ## The two negatives
##
## **`IntelSample*` — published by Intel, and not hardware.** Intel's own
## quote-verification library ships a sample quote and a sample trust
## root under `Src/AttestationApp/sampleData/tdx/`. The sample's chain
## terminates in a certificate whose subject is Intel's, character for
## character, which is self-signed, which is a certificate authority, and
## which the chain below it genuinely verifies under — and whose key is
## **not** Intel's. Its serial is `1a5865019d18b04e045af82d06c08550e20eb476`
## where the real root's is `22650cd65a9d3489f383b49552bf501b392706ac`.
##
## That is the strongest possible input for the root rule, and the reason
## is that nobody here made it: a complete, internally valid, correctly
## signed Intel-shaped hierarchy that must be refused for one reason and
## one reason only. The sample quote's TD report is all zeroes and its
## certification data is raw DER in root-first order rather than the PEM
## in leaf-first order every genuine quote carries, so it is also the
## input two structural rules needed.
##
## **`Impostor*` — minted here, and the only fabricated bytes in the
## file.** The genuine Sapphire Rapids quote with two substitutions: its
## PCK chain replaced by one generated in this workspace, carrying the
## same distinguished names and the same Intel platform extension with
## the same FMSPC, and its quoting-enclave report re-signed by the
## substituted leaf key. Everything else is the genuine quote's bytes.
## The result was checked to be *internally valid* before it was used as
## a negative — the chain links, every signature verifies, the report
## data still binds the genuine attestation key, and the quote signature
## still verifies under it — so it is refused because its root is not
## Intel's and for no other reason.
##
## Two negatives for one rule is not redundancy. Intel's proves the rule
## fires on a hierarchy this repository could not have arranged; the
## minted one proves it fires on a hierarchy that is otherwise
## indistinguishable from a genuine quote, down to the platform
## extension. Neither proves the pinned key is the RIGHT key — that is
## what the five routes at the top of this file are for, and the two
## claims rest on different evidence on purpose.
##
## ## What is missing, so the next agent does not assume otherwise
##
##   * No quote was produced on this host or by this organisation. No
##     part in reach supports this technology; these quotes were made by
##     somebody else's machines and published.
##   * No revoked PCK certificate that can be presented here. Intel's
##     platform list names a serial, so the serial reader has a real,
##     non-empty list to read — but no project publishes a certificate
##     holding that serial, and manufacturing the pair means holding
##     Intel's private key. The revocation RULE is exercised as a
##     function over the vendor's own list; the chain evaluator's
##     revocation REFUSAL has no offline input, and that is said out loud
##     rather than implied.
##   * No `qeIdentity` document whose TDX levels are anything but a
##     single `UpToDate` entry, in either vintage.
##   * No launch measurement is reproduced. Computing what `MRTD` and the
##     runtime measurement registers should be from an image is a
##     different piece of work and is not attempted here.

